WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.945
A public budget is a prospective policy document.

2
00:00:02.945 --> 00:00:10.277
It translates priorities into authority, staffing, contracts, capital work, and service expectations for a defined period.

3
00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:22.749
This episode adapts Kioko and Marlowe's chapter Budget Strategy, published by University of Washington Pressbooks under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 except where otherwise noted.

4
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:27.820
It condenses, paraphrases, and reorganizes the authors' explanatory prose.

5
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:34.125
Third-party cases, examples, figures, tables, quotations, and linked resources are excluded.

6
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:37.996
A sound process begins with a baseline forecast under current policy.

7
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:45.626
Separate continuing commitments from proposals, recurring resources from one-time resources, and operating needs from capital financing.

8
00:00:45.626 --> 00:00:49.778
Document economic, workload, compensation, and implementation assumptions.

9
00:00:49.778 --> 00:00:55.553
The legal budget controls spending, but useful budget information also connects dollars to work.

10
00:00:55.553 --> 00:01:02.231
Inputs show resources, outputs show units delivered, and outcomes show conditions the service seeks to improve.

11
00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:06.141
Targets should be few enough to manage and clear enough to audit.

12
00:01:06.141 --> 00:01:12.837
Participation and politics are part of budgeting because resource choices distribute benefits, burdens, and risk.

13
00:01:12.837 --> 00:01:20.186
Decision packages should show alternatives and service effects rather than invite debate over unexplained line-item changes.

14
00:01:20.186 --> 00:01:24.334
Reserves need stated purposes, target ranges, and replenishment rules.

15
00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:25.791
Timing shapes the choice.

16
00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:35.118
Information introduced before options narrow can change the baseline, while the same information arriving after commitments form may be treated as a disruption.

17
00:01:35.118 --> 00:01:42.054
A good calendar identifies forecast updates, proposal deadlines, public hearings, adoption, implementation, and review.

18
00:01:42.054 --> 00:01:46.368
It also names who may revise assumptions and who may authorize amendments.

19
00:01:46.368 --> 00:01:48.308
Torrance application segment.

20
00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:52.255
This original local exercise is separate from the textbook.

21
00:01:52.255 --> 00:02:00.415
It cites the City of Torrance Budget Documents and Transparent Torrance pages, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

22
00:02:00.415 --> 00:02:13.192
Their addresses are torrance C A dot gov slash Government slash Departments Offices slash Finance slash Budget Documents, and torrance C A dot gov slash Government slash Transparent Torrance.

23
00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:21.262
For a hypothetical budget briefing, start with the current adopted document and latest periodic review from the official budget index.

24
00:02:21.262 --> 00:02:31.077
Build a one-page decision table that separates baseline commitments, proposals, one-time resources, recurring resources, service effects, and implementation risks.

25
00:02:31.077 --> 00:02:36.437
Use the transparency page only to locate separately labeled dashboards and current tools.

26
00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:40.171
Preserve the fiscal year and adoption status on every exhibit.

27
00:02:40.171 --> 00:02:44.627
This exercise does not evaluate Torrance's adopted priorities or forecast.

28
00:02:44.627 --> 00:02:46.160
Adoption is not the end.

29
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:54.527
Monitor actual results against a seasonal plan, explain variances, update forecasts, and distinguish timing from structural change.

30
00:02:54.527 --> 00:02:59.765
Transparent amendments preserve authority and create a record of why policy moved.

31
00:02:59.765 --> 00:03:06.891
